<!--Render Target Documentation — CompositeRendering
  FQN: com.machineblocks.bml.target.render.documentation.CompositeRendering-->
<rdoc:CompositeRendering xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:rdoc="com.machineblocks.bml.target.render.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    Wrapper mb_block and nested children
  </is>
  <documentation>
    A composite block (non-empty composition) is a wrapper mb_block() with
    nested child calls, inside a typed module named by FqnNaming.

    Module body order:

    1. Native parameters via mb_param_* getters.
    2. Custom parameters via mb_param.
    3. assembly = mb_assembly(config, settings, size, direction)
       and renderGroups = mb_param_renderGroups(config, settings).
    4. Wrapper mb_block with id, base false, studs false, size, align, offset,
       direction.
    5. One child region per composition child: part_/group_/ns_ locals, then
       mb_block with id mb_block_id(...), render mb_group_render(...), size
       expression, and assembly helpers from AssemblyPatterns.

    Wrapper decision:

    - 0 children and custom processor logic — that logic renders; no wrapper.
    - 0 children and no custom processor — diagnostic MB_EMPTY_BLOCK.
    - exactly 1 child — no wrapper; the child is rendered directly.
    - 2 or more children — automatic wrapper. Children render inside it.

    The wrapper always sets base false and studs false, ignoring the block's
    own base and studs. The wrapper is a layout container. Geometry is the
    children. The block's base and studs are passed to the NativeBlock children.

        Cross.studs = true
          wrapper: studs false (processor)
          children: studs true (block property)

    Composite blocks do not need to declare base false or studs false for the
    wrapper. Those properties keep their meaning for the children.

    The automatic wrapper is only the outermost container, once per processor
    invocation. Nested blocks in the composition are real instances, not extra
    wrappers.

    Illustrative child settings (top part) include offset via
    mb_assembly_offset and baseCutoutType via mb_assembly_groove. Bottom parts
    include tongue via mb_assembly_tongue. Both forward baseAdjustment through
    mb_params_filter with that part's namespace.

  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="rdoc:SetRendering" relation="ReadNext" />
  </references>
</rdoc:CompositeRendering>
